antly and the  simple truth is that for the majority of  viewers homosexuality and references to  it are the exception and not the rule  and that has nothing to do with  prejudice or bigotry  that's just statistical fact a very high  percentage of the population run into  members of the LGBTQ community  infrequently enough that each time it  happens it is worth noting because it's  not normalized yet and therefore so is  any reference to that same community  that comes across in the entertainment  consumed by those same people so again  every time it's brought up it pings your  radar in a way that references to  heterosexual relationships just doesn't  do so that tackles the raw numbers but I  also want to address the conception that  the times that Bill does mention her  homosexuality and her preference for  women that it comes across as somehow  extremely forced or crowbarred in  because that's the other aspect that  people who like to bring bill up in the  argument use as the supposed smoking gun  this idea that there's no way a normal  person will be bringing this up in the  ways at the time she does that's how we  know it's crowbar Dean and pushing an  agenda so setting aside whether or not  the simple confirmation that a gay  person exists in a fictional world  should be taken as a political thing in  the first place seems as it's not a  belief system like someone repeatedly  bringing up the fact that they are  atheist or vegan or a socialist or well  but I'm tangent and getting off setting  that aside I just feel that this view  that bill is forcing this information  into the conversation where it doesn't  fit normally is very much skewed by  perspective because the people who make  this claim I would be willing to bet the  logic follows that well I don't have to  constantly tell people that I'm straight  why is she always bringing up the fact  that she's gay but the thing is the two  experiences don't equate because the  default assumption for most of us is 